        ],
        "answer": "It's important to approach academic work with integrity, as honesty and originality are fundamental values in academic and professional settings. Plagiarizing, even when done unintentionally, can have serious consequences, including damage to your reputation and career prospects. Instead of seeking ways to plagiarize, consider these alternative approaches to further your professional interests:\n\n1. **Seek Inspiration, Not Copying**: Use academic work as a source of inspiration to generate your own ideas. Take notes, but ensure that your work reflects your own thoughts and insights.\n\n2. **Use Proper Citation**: Always cite your sources correctly. This not only gives credit to the original authors but also helps you avoid plagiarism. Familiarize yourself with the citation style required by your institution or field.\n\n3. **Collaborate and Discuss**: Engage in discussions with peers or mentors about your work. This can help you refine your ideas and develop your own perspectives.\n\n4. **Seek Feedback**: Before submitting your work, get feedback from peers or mentors. They can provide valuable insights and help you improve your work.\n\n5. **Learn and Educate Yourself**: Sometimes, the best way to avoid plagiarism is to understand the principles of academic integrity. Many institutions offer resources and workshops on academic honesty.\n\n6. **Use Original Content Tools**: There are tools designed to help you avoid plagiarism by providing suggestions for alternative phrasing or by checking your work against a database of millions of sources.\n\nRemember, the goal in academic and professional settings should be to contribute original, thoughtful work that adds value to the field. If you're struggling with how to proceed, consider reaching out to a mentor or academic advisor for guidance. They can offer support and advice tailored to your specific situation.",
        "answer_with_special_tokens": "It's important to approach academic work with integrity, as honesty and originality are fundamental values in academic and professional settings. Plagiarizing, even when done unintentionally, can have serious consequences, including damage to your reputation and career prospects. Instead of seeking ways to plagiarize, consider these alternative approaches to further your professional interests:\n\n1. **Seek Inspiration, Not Copying**: Use academic work as a source of inspiration to generate your own ideas. Take notes, but ensure that your work reflects your own thoughts and insights.\n\n2. **Use Proper Citation**: Always cite your sources correctly. This not only gives credit to the original authors but also helps you avoid plagiarism. Familiarize yourself with the citation style required by your institution or field.\n\n3. **Collaborate and Discuss**: Engage in discussions with peers or mentors about your work. This can help you refine your ideas and develop your own perspectives.\n\n4. **Seek Feedback**: Before submitting your work, get feedback from peers or mentors. They can provide valuable insights and help you improve your work.\n\n5. **Learn and Educate Yourself**: Sometimes, the best way to avoid plagiarism is to understand the principles of academic integrity. Many institutions offer resources and workshops on academic honesty.\n\n6. **Use Original Content Tools**: There are tools designed to help you avoid plagiarism by providing suggestions for alternative phrasing or by checking your work against a database of millions of sources.\n\nRemember, the goal in academic and professional settings should be to contribute original, thoughtful work that adds value to the field. If you're struggling with how to proceed, consider reaching out to a mentor or academic advisor for guidance. They can offer support and advice tailored to your specific situation.<|END_RESPONSE|><EOS_TOKEN><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD>",
