        ],
        "answer": "Smashing a ball in tennis involves a combination of power, timing, and technique. Here are some tips to help you improve your smashing:\n\n1. Approach: Start by taking a strong, confident approach to the ball. Step forward with your non-dominant foot and bring your body into a balanced position.\n\n2. Timing: Time your approach so that you are in the right position to hit the ball at the right moment. Look for an open spot in the opponent's service box and aim to hit the ball when they are not covering it.\n\n3. Stance: Position your feet shoulder-width apart and shift your weight onto your front foot to generate power. Keep your back foot slightly back to help with balance and stability.\n\n4. Swing: Use your backswing to build power and generate speed. Focus on using your legs and hips to generate momentum, rather than just your arms.\n\n5. Contact: Aim to make contact with the ball at the right moment to maximize power and accuracy. Try to hit the ball at the sweet spot of the racket head for the best results.\n\n6. Follow-through: After making contact with the ball, follow through with your swing to complete the motion. This will help you generate more power and control.\n\n7. Practice: Like any skill, smashing requires practice to master. Spend time in the court working on your technique, timing, and power to improve your smashing game.",
        "answer_with_special_tokens": "Smashing a ball in tennis involves a combination of power, timing, and technique. Here are some tips to help you improve your smashing:\n\n1. Approach: Start by taking a strong, confident approach to the ball. Step forward with your non-dominant foot and bring your body into a balanced position.\n\n2. Timing: Time your approach so that you are in the right position to hit the ball at the right moment. Look for an open spot in the opponent's service box and aim to hit the ball when they are not covering it.\n\n3. Stance: Position your feet shoulder-width apart and shift your weight onto your front foot to generate power. Keep your back foot slightly back to help with balance and stability.\n\n4. Swing: Use your backswing to build power and generate speed. Focus on using your legs and hips to generate momentum, rather than just your arms.\n\n5. Contact: Aim to make contact with the ball at the right moment to maximize power and accuracy. Try to hit the ball at the sweet spot of the racket head for the best results.\n\n6. Follow-through: After making contact with the ball, follow through with your swing to complete the motion. This will help you generate more power and control.\n\n7. Practice: Like any skill, smashing requires practice to master. Spend time in the court working on your technique, timing, and power to improve your smashing game.<|END_RESPONSE|><EOS_TOKEN><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD><PAD>",
        "stop_diagnostics": {
